## Check Cluster Status

After this, check the status of the Flux system instance. `flux overlay status`
shows whether the broker on each compute node has connected to the *head node*,
and `flux resource status` shows the state of each node as seen by the
scheduler. All compute nodes should be listed as *avail*, while the *head node*
is listed as *exclude* since it has been withheld from scheduling:

```bash
flux overlay status
flux resource status
```

Continue after every compute node is free for jobs. To check:

```bash
flux resource list -s free
```

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Wait for the compute nodes to be free in Flux -->
<!-- ohpc_command for ((t=0; ; t++)) ; do -->
<!-- ohpc_command     [ "$(flux resource list -s free -no '{nnodes}')" -ge "${num_computes}" ] \ -->
<!-- ohpc_command         && { echo "Flux compute nodes: OK"; break; } -->
<!-- ohpc_command     (( t < 30 )) || { echo "Flux compute nodes: FAILED, not free after 300s"; flux resource status; exit 1; } -->
<!-- ohpc_command     echo "Waiting for the compute nodes to be free in Flux..." -->
<!-- ohpc_command     sleep 10 -->
<!-- ohpc_command done -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->

In case of additional Flux issues, ensure that `munge.service` and
`flux.service` are running on the affected nodes, that the hostname of each node
exactly matches its entry in the `hosts` array of
`/etc/flux/system/conf.d/system.toml`, and that the configuration and CURVE
certificate are identical across the nodes. You may also consult the [Flux
Administrator's
Guide](https://flux-framework.readthedocs.io/projects/flux-core/en/latest/guide/admin.html).
